## THE COMMON HOUSES — 42 total (22 Small, 14 Medium, 6 Large)

This is a finite, trackable pool for the whole floor — once robbed, a specific house doesn't regenerate its "robbable" status for the rest of the floor (the same household isn't hit twice). With four teams competing, houses genuinely run out over five days if robbery is popular.

### Robbing a House

| House Size | Rounds | Check | Base DC | Reward | Alertness (success) | Alertness (caught) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Small | 4 | Stealth or Sleight of Hand | 12 | 1 Small-tier item | +1 | +2 |
| Medium | 7 | Stealth or Sleight of Hand | 14 | 1 Medium-tier item | +2 | +4 |
| **Large** | 10 | Stealth or Sleight of Hand | 16 | 1 Large-tier item | **+5** | **+8**, and failure is *always* a Guard Chase (skip the d4 roll) |

**Large houses (6 total) belong to the town's wealthiest and most connected residents** — a guild official, the elder, retired merchants — and each has an active deterrent: a private guard, a watchdog, or an alarm arrangement. This is baked into the higher Alertness costs above and the automatic Guard Chase on failure.

### Alertness (party-wide, shared across ALL four teams — 0–9+)

| Alertness | State | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| 0–1 | Quiet | No DC change |
| 2–3 | Noticed | +2 to robbery DC |
| 4–5 | Watching | +4 to robbery DC |
| 6+ | Hunted | Common Houses locked to robbery for the rest of the day; attempting anyway is an automatic catch |

Decays −2 every morning (Step 1 of the morning sequence), before Guard Investigation is resolved.

### Getting Caught

| d4 | Consequence |
|---|---|
| 1 | **Guard Chase** — Stealth or Athletics DC 10 + current tier's DC bonus. Failure: stall confiscated 1 day + Reputation −3. |
| 2 | **On-the-spot fine** — lose gold equal to 2× the house tier's base value. Reputation −1. |
| 3 | **Marked** — Reputation −2; further robbery Alertness gain doubled for the rest of today. |
| 4 | **Iron Ledger notices** — Syndicate Pressure +1, and +1 to that team's Grudge Track (see Syndicate section). |

